ENETC-4 with i.mx95
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Note: Refer 'Cross compiled toolchain For ARM64' section in nxp/README
	$ meson arm64-build --cross-file config/arm/arm64_imx_linux_gnu_gcc -Dexamples=all
	$ ninja -C arm64-build

  Generic steps on i.MX95 before running dpdk:
  --------------------------------------------
  1. Bring up the board with proper images and set "iommu.passthrough=1" in mmcargs:
        setenv mmcargs <user-specific-params> isolcpus=1-5 iommu.passthrough=1;
  2. Set "fdtfile" on uboot with default dtb:
	setenv fdtfile imx95-19x19-evk.dtb
  3. Boot the board
	boot
  4. Load the non-cacheable module. (optional on i.MX95 and i.MX943)
	insmod kpage_ncache.ko
	Note: On i.MX95 and i.MX943, the enetc4 driver uses cacheable BD memory by
	      default (no kpage_ncache.ko required). Load kpage_ncache.ko only if you
	      intend to use non-cacheable BD memory via the "nc=1" devarg (see below).
  5. Manually allocate hugepages
	echo 512 > /proc/sys/vm/nr_hugepages

  Multi-queue support:
  -------------------

  Devlink utilty in Linux can be used to configure multiple queues in init
  process of PF.

  The number of rings allocated to each SI is at least 1.

  Run below commands to configure multiple queues:
  ------------------------------------------------
  1. Example to set the number of rings of PF to 1, VF0 to 6 and VF1 to 1:
	$ ./devlink dev param set pci/0002:00:10.0 name si_num_rings cmode driverinit value 0106
	Note: Here,
	      -> 0002:00:10.0 is PF PCI address.
	      -> The 'value' is a string type value.
		 Numerically higher VFs have lower bytes of the string, as an example,
		 if one ENETC supports 2 VFs, and the string is set to "304" or
		 "0304", then the ring allocation for each VF is shown below.
		 VF0 = 0x04
		 VF1 = 0x03

		Note that the number of rings of PF is not explicitly presented in the
		string, because the remaining number of rings is reserved for PF.

  2. Run devlink reload command to apply the new configuration.
	$ ./devlink dev reload pci/0002:00:10.0
  3. Verify the value of si_num_rings
	$ devlink dev param show pci/0002:00:10.0 name si_num_rings
  Example output:
	pci/0002:00:10.0:
	name si_num_rings type driver-specific
	values:
		cmode driverinit value 0106

  Running VFs in DPDK (Linux kernel on ARM core owned ENETC PF interface)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Note:
	Max 2 VFs are supported per ENETC, follow the same steps as below to run DPDK on
	them.

  1. Run the below steps to bind VFs to dpdk:
	a. If multiple queues are to be used, follow above section before creating VFs.
	   Run below command to create a VF:
		$ echo 1 > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0002\:00\:00.0/sriov_numvfs
		Note: Use 'dpdk-devbind.py -s' to identify the PCI address of 1g and 10g ports
		      like 0002:00:00.0 in the above example.
	b. Check dmesg logs to identify the PCI address of VF created
		Example kernel logs for VF:
		[ 2002.156985] fsl_enetc_vf 0002:00:12.0: enabling device (0000 -> 0002)
		[ 2002.517997] uio_pci_generic 0002:00:12.0: enabling device (0000 -> 0002)
		[ 2002.543684] uio_pci_generic 0002:00:12.0: No IRQ assigned to device: no support for interrupts?		
	c. Use dpdk-devbind.py to bind VF to dpdk:
		dpdk-devbind.py -b uio_pci_generic <vf-pci-addr>

		or

		modprobe igb_uio
		dpdk-devbind.py -b igb_uio <vf-pci-addr>

		As per the above logs, 0002:00:12.0 will be the <vf-pci-addr>
		Note: only uio_pci_generic and igb_uio frameworks are supported to access device in DPDK.
		      Use "igb_uio" to bind VF to DPDK to get link status change notifications. igb_uio is a separate
		      kernel module, so make sure to load it first using modprobe or insmod command
		      before device binding.
		Note: To test the ENETC4 VF with the vfio-pci framework in VFIO No-IOMMU mode on
		      i.MX95, follow the dedicated guide nxp/README_imx95_enetc_vf_vfio. It documents
		      the required kernel config, bootargs, and the imx95.dtsi SMMU change needed for
		      vfio-pci and VSI-PSI messaging to work.

  2. Enable trust for the specified VF user by specifying the PF eth interface.
	ip link set <eth-interface> vf 0 trust on
	Note: Check eth interface using:
              eg. ./dpdk-devbind.py -s [In below o/p eth0 is the PF eth interface]
                0002:00:00.0 'Device e101' if=eth0 drv=fsl_enetc4 unused=vfio-pci,uio_pci_generic
  3. Run:
	$ ./dpdk-l2fwd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1
	$ ./dpdk-l3fwd -l 1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 -P --config="(0,0,1)"
	$ ./dpdk-testpmd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x1
	$ ./dpdk-ip_fragmentation -l 1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1
	$ ./dpdk-ip_reassembly -l 1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1

	Examples to run multiqueue:
	$ ./dpdk-l3fwd -l 0-5 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 -P --config="(0,0,1) (0,1,2) (0,2,3) (0,3,4) (0,4,5) (0,5,0)"
	$ ./dpdk-testpmd -l 0-5 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x1 --rxq=5 --txq=5

	Note: Use option "--no-lsc-interrupt" in dpdk-testpmd when using uio_pci_generic framework to avoid
	      link interrupts registration related errors.

	TSO (TCP/UDP segmentation offload) on VF:
	TSO is offloaded per Tx queue. Use the testpmd csum engine to drive it:
	$ ./dpdk-testpmd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x1 --tx-offloads=0x00008000
		testpmd> port stop 0
		testpmd> port config 0 tx_offload tcp_tso on
		testpmd> csum set ip hw 0
		testpmd> csum set tcp hw 0
		testpmd> csum set udp hw 0
		testpmd> tso set 256 0
		testpmd> port start 0
		testpmd> set fwd csum
		testpmd> start
	Note: For UDP segmentation, also enable the udp_tso offload:
	      testpmd> port config 0 tx_offload udp_tso on

	LRO (Large Receive Offload) via hardware RSC:
	LRO is offloaded through the ENETC Receive Segment Coalesce (RSC) engine
	and is enabled per port when the TCP LRO Rx offload is requested. RSC
	requires the FCS to be stripped, so it cannot be combined with KEEP_CRC.
	$ ./dpdk-testpmd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x1 --rx-offloads=0x00004000
		testpmd> port stop 0
		testpmd> port config 0 rx_offload tcp_lro on
		testpmd> port config all max-lro-pkt-size 1200
		testpmd> port start 0
		testpmd> set fwd rxonly
		testpmd> start
	Note: Coalesced frames are delivered with the RTE_MBUF_F_RX_LRO flag set;
	      use "show port stats 0" to observe reduced packet counts.

  PSI-VSI messaging:
  -----------------
  The hardware supports VF to send messages to PF by the VSI-TO-PSI
  messaging mechanism for supporting features not under VF's control.

  Currently the following features have been supported for VF using kernel PF using this mechanism:
    * VF primary MAC based filtering
    * Enable/Disable promiscuous mode
    * Enable/Disable allmulti MAC mode
    * Get link status
    * Get link speed
    * MAC Exact Match filtering
    * VLAN Exact Match filtering
    * Enable/Disable VLAN promiscous mode
    * Link status interrupts

  1. Follow the above steps to run dpdk-testpmd.
  2. Users can control the timeout and delay time counters with env. variables [optional]
	export ENETC4_VSI_WAIT_DELAY_UPDATE=<val>
	export ENETC4_VSI_WAIT_TIMEOUT_UPDATE=<val>
	Note: Using this user has control over waiting time for PSI to process message
	      sent by VSI.
  3. Enable trust for the specified VF user by specifying the PF eth interface.
	ip link set <eth-interface> vf 0 trust on
	Note: Check eth interface using:
              eg. ./dpdk-devbind.py -s [In below o/p eth0 will be the PF eth interface]
		0002:00:00.0 'Device e101' if=eth0 drv=fsl_enetc4 unused=vfio-pci,uio_pci_generic
  4. These features can be tested using dpdk-testpmd using its below runtime functions:
	-> mac_addr set <port_id> <valid_mac_address> /* Set Primary MAC */
	-> set promisc <port_id> off/on		      /* Enable/Disable promiscuous mode */
	-> set allmulti <port_id> off/on	      /* Enable/Disable allmulti MAC mode */
	-> show port info <port_id>		      /* Get link status/speed */

  To add MAC adddress exact match entry:
  -------------------------------------
	1. Disable the promiscuous mode to test the MAC entries filtering.
		-> set promisc <port_id> off
	2. Run below command to add exact match MAC address entry:
		-> mac_addr add <port_id> <valid_mac_address>

  To add/remove VLAN exact match entry:
  ------------------------------------
  Run below commands on dpdk-testpmd cmdline:
	1. Run below command to disable VLAN promisc mode & set VLAN filter mode for the port:
		-> vlan set filter on <port_id>
		Note: By default, VLAN promisc mode is enabled.
	2. Once the VLAN promisc is off, we can add exact match vlan entries:
		-> rx_vlan add <vlan_id> <port_id>
	3. If required the entry can be removed using below command:
		-> rx_vlan rm <vlan_id> <port_id>
	4. To enable promisc mode & unset VLAN filter mode for the port:
		-> vlan set filter off <port_id>

  Note:
	Currently, maximum MAC and VLAN address exact match filter table entries that
	can be added are 4 per ENETC.

  Link status interrupts:
  -----------------------
  DPDK is dependent on igb_uio module instead of uio_pci_generic for interrupts.

  Run below commands to bind the device to igb_uio:
	1. insmod igb_uio.ko or modprobe igb_uio
	2. echo igb_uio > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0002\:00\:12.0/driver_override
	3. echo 0002:00:12.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/fsl_enetc_vf/unbind
	4. echo 0002:00:12.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/igb_uio/bind

  Run dpdk-testpmd for checking status interrupts:
	$ ./dpdk-testpmd -l 2,3 --log-level=*:debug -n 1 -- -i --portmask 0x1

  or
  Run the dpdk-link_status_interrupt application:
	$ ./dpdk-link_status_interrupt -l 0-3 -n 1 -a <vf1-pci-addr> -a <vf2-pci-addr> -- -p 0x3

  Open a new terminal and try to change the link status using either below commands
  or attach and de-attach cable:

	$ ifconfig eth1 down
	$ ifconfig eth1 up

  Note: Here eth1 is the PF interface.

  User will observe link change notification on console.
  It may take few seconds to get the notification of link up.

  VF1 validation:
  ---------------
  DPDK support ENETC0(1G RGMII) VF1 and ENETC2(10G UXSGMII) VF1 interfaces.
  PF and VF0 must be owned by kernel while testing.

  1. Refer 'Steps to run VF(s) in dpdk' step 1-5.
  2. Run devbind.py script for device id
	$ ./dpdk-devbind.py -s
  3. Create 2 VFs, one for dpdk and one for kernel
	$  echo 2 > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0002\:00\:10.0/sriov_numvfs
  4. Check dmesg logs to identify the PCI address of VF1 created
	Example kernel logs for VF:
        	[ 107.122861] pci 0002:00:12.0: [1131:ef00] type 00 class 0x020001
		[ 107.230531] fsl_enetc_vf 0002:00:12.0: enabling device (0000 -> 0002)
		[ 107.232173] pci 0002:00:14.0: [1131:ef00] type 00 class 0x020001
		[ 107.342547] fsl_enetc_vf 0002:00:14.0: enabling device (0000 -> 0002)
  5. Use dpdk-devbind.py to bind VF1 to dpdk:
	$ dpdk-devbind.py -b uio_pci_generic <vf1-pci-addr>
	As per the above logs, 0002:00:14.0 will be the <vf1-pci-addr>
  6. Enable trust for the specified VF user by specifying the PF eth interface.
	ip link set <eth-interface> vf 0 trust on
	Note: Check eth interface using:
              eg. ./dpdk-devbind.py -s [In below o/p eth0 is the PF eth interface]
                   0002:00:00.0 'Device e101' if=eth0 drv=fsl_enetc4 unused=vfio-pci,uio_pci_generic
  7. Run:
	$ ./dpdk-l2fwd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1
	$ ./dpdk-l3fwd -l 1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 -P --config="(0,0,1)"


  Running DPDK as Non-Root User with sudo permission:
  --------------------------------------------------

  Step 1: Create and Configure Non-Root User
	1. Create a new user and give sudo permissions:

	$ adduser dpdk_user
	$ visudo
		Add the following line to give sudo permission:

		dpdk_user ALL=(ALL) ALL

		Save and exit.

	Note: This is a one-time configuration step.

  Step 2: Setup Board for Non-Root User (as Root)

	1. Please refer to the following sections in this README and follow the steps as the "root" user to set up the board for non-root user:

		-> Generic steps on i.MX95 before running DPDK: Load kpage_ncache.ko (if required) and configure hugepages.
		-> Multi-queue: Configure multiple queues.
		-> Running VFs in DPDK: Bind VFs to uio_pci_generic for DPDK.

	2. Switch to the non root user:
		$ su dpdk_user

  Step 3: Run DPDK Application using Sudo
	$ sudo dpdk-l2fwd -l 0,1 -- -p 0x3 -T 0

  TSN-QBV:
  -------

  - IEEE 802.1Qbv allows for time-based scheduling of network traffic.
  - The Gate Control List (GCL) is a fundamental component of the IEEE 802.1Qbv protocol.
  - The GCL (Gate C Logic) entries decide the gate open/close timings.
  - The queues are allowed to transmit only when the gate is open.
  - The GCL operates in a cyclic manner, repeating its sequence of entries periodically.

  Follow the below steps to test TSN-QBV scenario for ENETC:

  1. Configuring multiple queues for VF0

  Using below steps we will be able to configure 5 queues for VF0.
  Maximum queues for VF0 can be 5 because minimum 2 is required for PF for using
  tc command to configure gate scheduling and 1 is reserved for VF1.

  $ devlink dev param set pci/0002:00:10.0 name si_num_rings cmode driverinit value 0105
  $ devlink dev reload pci/0002:00:10.0
  $ devlink dev param show pci/0002:00:10.0 name si_num_rings

  2. VF creating/binding

  $ echo 1 > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0002\:00\:10.0/sriov_numvfs
  $ dpdk-devbind.py -b uio_pci_generic 0002:00:12.0
  $ ip link set eth1 vf 0 trust on

  3. Configure QBV rule using tc command:

  Using the below command, the gate scheduling is configured.
   - Each queue is configured to a particular TC.
   - In below command, gate is configured open for a particular queue for
     100 microsec and then all queue gates are closed for 50 microsec in between
     every gate scheduling.
   - Configuring all gates closed for some time can be beneficial when analyzing
     the captured data packets.
   - The priority for each queue is configured by user in DPDK using devargs argument.
   - Although num_tc and map define only two PF queues, the GCL includes entries
     for multiple queues, including VF queues (e.g., VF0).
     This is intentional so that the GCL can be effective for DPDK applications.
   - In the tc command, queue IDs are referenced using the mask values (e.g., S 1, S 2, etc.).
     These map directly to TX queues in DPDK when the GCL is applied.
     For eg. S 1 corresponds to the txq0 in DPDK, S 2 maps to txq1, and so on.

  For more details on tc qdisc and its usage, refer to the tc qdisc man page:
  Run man tc in your terminal for details.

  $ tc qdisc replace dev eth1 parent root handle 200 taprio num_tc 2 map 0 1  queues 1@0 1@1 base-time 10000 sched-entry S 1 100000 sched-entry S 0 50000 sched-entry S 2 100000  sched-entry S 0 50000 sched-entry S 4 100000 sched-entry S 0 50000  sched-entry S 8 100000 sched-entry S 0 50000  sched-entry S 10 100000  sched-entry S 0 50000  sched-entry S 20 100000 sched-entry S 0 50000  sched-entry S 40 100000 sched-entry S 0 50000 sched-entry S 80 100000 flags 0x2

  4. Check if the rule is properly implemented:
     $ tc -d -s qdisc show dev eth1 root

  5. Run the test-pmd command:

  - The priority for each queue is configured by user in DPDK using devargs
    argument as used in the below command.
  - The priorities for each queue are separated by '|' in devargs.

  $ ./dpdk-testpmd -l 0-3 -n 1 -a 0002:00:12.0,enetc4_txq_prior="1|2|3|4|5" --
     -i --nb-ports=1 --nb-cores=3 --forward-mode=txonly --txq=3
     --txonly_vlan_multiq_enable --txpkts=1500

  In tc qdisc GCL: Queue IDs (S 1, S 2, etc.) are scheduled for specific time slices.
  In DPDK: These queue IDs map to transmit queues as follows:
	S 1 -> txq0
	S 2 -> txq1
	S 4 -> txq2, and so on.

  6. Capture the packets on spirent and analyse the wireshark output.

  The output should be the way the rule is configured using tc command.

  As per the above tc command, the packets should be such that:
  VLAN priority 0 is seen for 100 microsec, then a break of 50 microsec should be observed
  then again for 100 microsec VLAN priority 1 is seen, then a break of 50 microsec
  and so on..

  CBS shaper (IEEE 802.1Qav) with a DPDK VF:
  -----------------------------------------

  - The ENETC Tx Credit-Based Shaper (CBS) can rate-limit traffic from a DPDK VF
    driven by the enetc4_vf PMD. See nxp/README_enetc4_vf_cbs for the full guide.

  Running ENETC PFs in DPDK:
  -------------------------

  1. Load the enetc4_uio module to enable DPDK functionality for external PF interfaces.
        $ modprobe enetc4_uio.ko

  2. Bind the PFs to the DPDK-compatible enetc4_uio driver
        $ dpdk-devbind.py -b enetc4_uio 0002:00:10.0

  3. Run dpdk-application:
        $ dpdk-l2fwd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 -T 0
        $ dpdk-l3fwd -l 1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 --config="(0,0,1)"
        $ dpdk-testpmd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x1 --forward-mode=mac
                testpmd> set promisc all off
                testpmd> start

   Optional: Test with non-cacheable BD memory (requires kpage_ncache.ko loaded):
        $ dpdk-l2fwd -l 2,3 -a 0002:00:02.0,nc=1 -- -p 0x1 -T 0

  Notes:
  -----

  -> i.MX95 supports jumbo frame, device supports maximum frame size to 2000 Bytes.
  -> Application can receive up to 2000 Bytes size frames.
  -> Availability of Multiple queues is dependent on ENETC PF driver (Arm core kernel driver/M7 core driver).
  -> RSS will be enabled on using multiple queues.
	=> RSS secret key is random key.
	=> Enabled RSS hash functions are IP source address, IP destination address, UDP/TCP source port and UDP/TCP destination port.
	=> Receive queues are equally configured in RSS indirection table to receive flows.
  -> Hardware Packet type parsing for supported packets is enabled by default.
  -> Checksum validation offload is enabled by default.
  -> Only 1G Intel Gigabit PCI-E Network Adapter EXPI9301CTBLK is supported.
  -> Only ENETC 10G interface is supported with M7 image
  -> ip_fragmentation can receive only up to 2000 bytes fragments.
  -> ip_reassembly can send only up to 2000 bytes reassembled packet.
  -> Link status change notifications are supported with igb_uio framework only.
  -> It may takes few seconds to get link status UP notification.

===============================================================================
DPDK on i.mx943:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  For running external ENETC PF ports in DPDK:
  --------------------------------------------

  1. Set the below dtb file:
	uboot> setenv fdtfile imx943-evk.dtb;

  2. Load the enetc4_uio module to enable DPDK functionality for external PF interfaces.
	$ modprobe enetc4_uio.ko

  3. Bind the PFs to the DPDK-compatible enetc4_uio driver
	$ dpdk-devbind.py -b enetc4_uio 0001:01:08.0

  4. Load the non-cacheable module: (optional on i.MX943 and i.MX95)
	$ modprobe kpage_ncache.ko
	Note: kpage_ncache.ko is not required by default. The enetc4 driver uses
	      cacheable BD memory. Load it only if testing with nc=1 devarg:
	      $ dpdk-l2fwd -l 2,3 -a 0001:01:08.0,nc=1 -- -p 0x1 -T 0

  5. Allocate hugepages for DPDK memory
	$ echo 512 > /proc/sys/vm/nr_hugepages

  6. Run dpdk-application:
	$ dpdk-l2fwd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 -T 0
	$ dpdk-l3fwd -l 1 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 --config="(0,0,1)"
	$ dpdk-testpmd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x1 --forward-mode=mac
		testpmd> set promisc all off
		testpmd> start

  Note:
	Only the internal ENETC port supports VF, which is connected to the switch
	CPU port. External ENETC ports do not support VF.

  1. Set the below dtb file to enable switch port:
	uboot> setenv fdtfile imx943-evk-sgmii.dtb;
  2. Create and configure the bridge with two switch ports
	$ ip link add name br0 type bridge
	$ ip link set dev swp0 master br0
	$ ip link set dev swp1 master br0
	$ ip link set dev br0 up
	$ ip link set dev swp0 up
	$ ip link set dev swp1 up
  3. Enable 2 Virtual Functions (VFs):
	$ echo 2 > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:00.0/sriov_numvfs
  4. Bind the VFs to the DPDK-compatible driver
	$ dpdk-devbind.py -b uio_pci_generic 0000:00:08.0
	$ dpdk-devbind.py -b uio_pci_generic 0000:00:10.0
  5. Enable trusted mode for both VFs:
	$ ip link set eth0 vf 0 trust on
	$ ip link set eth0 vf 1 trust on
  6. Allocate hugepages for DPDK memory
	$ echo 512 > /proc/sys/vm/nr_hugepages
  7. Load the non-cacheable module:
	$ modprobe kpage_ncache.ko
  8. Add static forwarding entries to the bridge's forwarding database (FDB)
	$ bridge fdb add 02:00:00:00:00:00 dev swp0 master static
	$ bridge fdb add 02:00:00:00:00:01 dev swp1 master static

	Note: 	These mean, that if a packet has destination MAC 02:00:00:00:00:00,
		the bridge will forward it out swp0. If the destination is
		02:00:00:00:00:01,it will go out via swp1.
		For dpdk-l2fwd, The destination MAC address is replaced by
		02:00:00:00:00:TX_PORT_ID hence this is set here. For dpdk-l3fwd
		we can add static mac address via cmdline. dpdk-testpmd sets the
		same MAC when run in mac mode.
  9. Run dpdk-application:

	$ dpdk-l2fwd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -p 0x3 -T 0
	$ dpdk-l3fwd -l 1 -n 1 -- -p 0x3 --config="(0,0,1), (1,0,1)"
	$ dpdk-testpmd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x3 --forward-mode=mac
		testpmd> set promisc all off
		testpmd> start
  10. To run dpdk-application with v2X fast crypto engine

	$ ./dpdk-test --vdev 'v2x_fce' --log-level=6
	  >>cryptodev_v2x_fce_autotest

  'dpdk-test-crypto-perf' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  'test-crypto-perf' is part of standard DPDK build process. The binary is generated
  in (./<target>/build/app/test-crypto-perf/) folder.
  A script 'v2x_fce_perf_test.sh' is added in nxp/ folder to run all supported cases
  in one go.

  Use the script as (for V2X_FCE)
  $./v2x_fce_perf_test.sh v2x_fce

  Some default parameters are added in the script which can be modified as per
  requirement.

  NOTE:
  ----
  Currently, The below test cases are not supported on I.MX943:
	-> Allmulti promisc mode
	-> Get Link status of VF
	-> MAC address exact match filter
	-> Multi queue
	-> Link status interrupts
	-> Jumbo frame
	-> dpdk-ip-fragmentation
	-> dpdk-reassembly

===============================================================================
  'dpdk-testpmd' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  'dpdk-testpmd' is part of standard DPDK build process. The binary is generated in
  the (./<target>/app/) folder. On a rootfs having DPDK binaries, the binary
  would be placed in ($PREFIX_SCRIPT_PATH/).

  Execute following commands to run DPDK testpmd

  $./dpdk-testpmd -l 0-3 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x3 --nb-cores=2

  Above command uses a port mask of first two ports. On DPAA and DPAA2 platforms
  this is defined by their respective configuration. See note above.

  This command would start the testpmd application in interactive mode, starting
  a shell for accepting further commands. For e.g:

  testpmd> show port info all

  Above command can be used to view all the ports which the framework has
  identified, with their detailed information.

  testpmd> <tab>

  On the `testpmd` prompt, <tab> key can be used for command completion as well
  as command help.

  On i.mx8mm platform
  ./dpdk-testpmd -l 1,2 -n 1 --vdev='net_enetfec' -- -i --portmask=0x1 --nb-ports=1 --forward-mode=io

  In TX-only mode:
	./dpdk-testpmd -l 1,2 -n 1 --vdev='net_enetfec' -- -i --portmask=0x1 --nb-ports=1 --forward-mode=tx_only

  'dpdk-test' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Test application can be used to do functional testing of the drivers and libraries.
  The binary is tested for various algos and protocols in crypto pmds. 'dpdk-test' binary
  is generated at arm64-build/app/test while compiling DPDK.

  Run binary as
  $./dpdk-test --log-level=7

  (for DPAA_SEC)
  RTE>> cryptodev_dpaa_sec_autotest

  or (for DPAA2_SEC)
  RTE>> cryptodev_dpaa2_sec_autotest

  Run the 'autotest.py' script as
  $ python3 app/test/autotest.py ./dpdk-test arm64-dpaa-linuxapp-gcc

  NOTE: For 'autotest.py' script, all the dependent 'app/test/*.py' scripts and
	'python-pexpect' package is required on the board.
	$ sudo apt-get install python3-pexpect

  'dpdk-test-crypto-perf' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  'test-crypto-perf' is part of standard DPDK build process. The binary is generated
  in (./<target>/build/app/test-crypto-perf/) folder.
  A script 'crypto_perf_test.sh' is added in nxp/ folder to run all supported cases
  in one go.

  Use the script as (for DPAA_SEC)
  $./crypto_perf_test.sh dpaa_sec
  or (for DPAA2_SEC)
  $./crypto_perf_test.sh dpaa2_sec

  Some default parameters are added in the script which can be modified as per
  requirement.


  Layer-2 Forwarding 'l2fwd' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Execute following commands to run the 'l2fwd' example application:

  $ ./dpdk-l2fwd -l 0 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 -q 1 -P

  OR

  $ ./dpdk-l2fwd -l 0,1 -n 1 -- -p 0x3 -q 1 -P

  In the above commands, the port mask has been modified to support first and
  first two ports, respectively. Also, the Core mask has been modified to
  execute on Core 0 and (Core 0 + Core 1), respectively.

  NOTE: For best performance, Core 0 should not be used for performing DPDK
        I/O. This is because large number of system services as well as some
        hardwired interrupts lines are services by Core 0.

        It is also advisable to use 'isolcpus=<>' when booting Linux Kernel.
        Value passed to this parameter should be the CPUs planned to be used
        for DPDK applications.


  Layer-2 Crypto Forwarding 'l2fwd-crypto' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Execute following commands to run the 'l2fwd-crypto' example application:

  #cipher mode only
  $ ./dpdk-l2fwd-crypto -l 0 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 --chain CIPHER_ONLY --cipher_op ENCRYPT --cipher_algo aes-cbc --cipher_key 00:01:02:03:04:05:06:07:08:09:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f

  #auth mode only
  $ ./dpdk-l2fwd-crypto -l 0 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 -q 1 --chain HASH_ONLY --auth_algo sha1-hmac --auth_op GENERATE --auth_key_random_size 64

  #cipher-auth mode
  $ ./dpdk-l2fwd-crypto -l 0-3 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 --chain CIPHER_HASH --cipher_op ENCRYPT --cipher_algo aes-cbc --auth_algo sha1-hmac --auth_op GENERATE --auth_key_random_size 64

  For caam_jr mode:
	use  --vdev 'crypto_caam_jr1'
  For openssl mode:
	use --vdev 'crypto_openssl'
  For ipsec_mb mode:
	use --vdev 'crypto_aesni_mb0' # To run various algos combinations e.g SNOW-ZUC, ZUC-SNOW
	use --vdev 'crypto_snow3g'  # To run only SNOW3G cases.
	use --vdev 'crypto_zuc' # To run only ZUC cases.
  For armv8_crypto mode:
	use --vdev 'crypto_armv8'
  For v2x_fce mode (valid for iMX943 only):
	use --vdev 'v2x_fce'

  You need to disable the DPAA_HW SEC on DPAA devices by to use any other sec driver.
   export DPAA_SEC_DISABLE=1
   or use the export DPSECI_COUNT=0 for dpaa2 before creating the dprc.
  NOTE: Platforms with CAAM JR support need the kernel support and they need
	to run following commands to assign job rings to userspace.
	#Find the address for job ring
		ls /sys/bus/platform/devices/*.jr
	#leave one and assign remaining rings to userspace drivers.
		echo 1720000.jr > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/caam_jr/unbind
		echo 1720000.jr > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/fsl-jr-uio/bind

		echo 1730000.jr > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/caam_jr/unbind
		echo 1730000.jr > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/fsl-jr-uio/bind

	You will need to use --vdev as "crypto_caam_jr0" "crypto_caam_jr1"
	to use these rings.

  Layer-2 Policer Forwarding 'l2fwd-policer' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Execute following commands to run the 'l2fwd-policer' example application:

  Basic command with least parameters, running on single core(core 1) and handle single port:

  $ ./dpdk-l2fwd-policer -l 1 -- -p 0x1 -P

  Application processes all the RXQs belonging to all TCs(MAX_TCS x MAX_QUEUES), the flows are distributed
  by vlan ID, vlan priority and IPv4 address.

  Static configuration by arguments:
  --color: configure policer color (color_aware, color_bliend), default: color_aware.
  --policer_unit: configure policer unit (bytes_l2, bytes_l3, frames), default: bytes_l2.
  --default_color: configure policer default color (red, yellow or green), default: green.
  --red_action: configure action of packet marked as red (red drop or red pass), default: red drop.
  --cir: NUM in bytes/frames as selected, default: 2000.
  --cbs: NUM in bytes/frames as selected, default: 10240.
  --pir: NUM in bytes/frames as selected, default: 2000.
  --pbs: NUM in bytes/frames as selected, default: 20480.
  --meter_action: Configure meter flow action type (meter_mark, meter, pass), default: meter_mark,
		which directly gets profile and(or) policy to configure flow action dynamically.
  --vlan_id Vlan ID selected to configure QoS flow, default: 100.
  --queue_config: Configure (port,queue,core) to receive packets by scheduler per core, it's mandatary
		for running on multiple cores.
  --miss_drop: Drop packets missed on QoS or TC' flow table, default is to receive in default TC/queue.
  --tx_multi_ports: Send packets to different ports by single burst transmission,
		disable by 0 and enable by 1, default: 1.
  --flow_table_level: Create 2-level flow table by 2 and 1-level table by 1, For 2-level flow table,
		vlan items are used to distribute traffic to TCs and IPv4 items are used to distribute
		traffic to flows(queues), default: 2.
  --print_stat: Print port and TC traffic statistics, default: disabled.
  --s_rss: Distribute TC's traffic by RSS, default is FS distribution.
  --rx_sch_mode: Select RX schedule mode (pull or push), default: push.
  --xstat: Print xstat (dev, mac, both or off), default: dev.

  Dynamic configuration by runtime commands:
  Enter Port ID to update: range[0, enter to skip]:
  Enter miss TC ID of port0 to update:(default=7) range[0 ~ 7 drop >= 8, enter to skip]:

  Enter QoS flow index of port0 to update item(s): range[0 ~ 7, enter to skip]:
  Enter index of QoS item of port0 to update item(s): [eth/src:0, vlan/tci:1, ipv4/src:2, enter to skip]:

  Enter TC ID to update: range[0 ~ 7, enter to skip]:
  Enter miss queue ID of port0-tc0 to update:(default=7) range[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7,  drop != any enter to skip]:
  Enter flow ID of port0-tc0 to update item(s): range[0 ~ 7, enter to skip]:
  Enter index of flow item for port0-tc0-flow2 update: [eth/src:0, vlan/tci:1, ipv4/src:2, enter to skip]:
  Enter flow ID of port0-tc0 to update action: range[0 ~ 7, enter to skip]:
  Enter queue ID to update:(default=3) range[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7,  enter to skip]:

  Enter cir to update:(default=2000):
  Enter cbs to update:(default=10240):
  Enter pir to update:(default=2000):
  Enter pbs to update:(default=20480):
  Enter red action to update:(default=drop):

  Layer-3 Forwarding 'l3fwd' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Execute following commands to run 'l3fwd' application

  For 1 core - 1 Port, 1 queue per port =>

  $ ./dpdk-l3fwd -l 0 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 --config="(0,0,0)"

  For 4 core - 2 Port, 2 queue per port =>

  $ ./dpdk-l3fwd -l 0-3 -n 1 -- -p 0x3 -P --config="(0,0,0),(0,1,1),(1,0,2),(1,1,3)"

  For 4 core - 2 Port with dest mac =>

  $ ./dpdk-l3fwd -l 0-3 -n 1 -- -p 0x3 -P --config="(0,0,0),(0,1,1),(1,0,2),(1,1,3)" --eth-dest=0,11:11:11:11:11:11 --eth-dest=1,00:00:00:11:11:11

  For 8 core - 4 Port with 4 queue per port =>

  $ ./dpdk-l3fwd -l 0-7 -n 1 -- -p 0x3 -P --config="(0,0,0),(0,1,1),(1,0,2),(1,1,3),(2,0,4),(2,1,5),(3,0,6),(3,1,7)"

  Hereafter, use the packet generator (Spirent, for example) to send traffic
  streams with below configuration of the destination IP address in the frames
  being sent:

      For traffic to port 1: 198.18.0.0/24
      For traffic to port 2: 198.18.1.0/24
      For traffic to port 3: 198.18.2.0/24
      For traffic to port 4: 198.18.3.0/24

  Layer-3 Forwarding 'l3fwd' Application using traffic bifurcation
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  L3fwd application has been modified to support traffic bifurcation, where traffic can be
  bifurcated such that some traffic goes to the kernel and other to DPDK using DPDMUX.
  Separate rules can be configured for this bifurcation. Below are some sample commands
  on LX2:

  1. Configure DPDMUX to have two DPNI's, one with kernel and other with user-space

  $ ls-addni --no-link
  $ source $PREFIX_SCRIPT_PATH/dpaa2/dynamic_dpl.sh dpmac.5
  $ echo dprc.2 > /sys/bus/fsl-mc/drivers/vfio-fsl-mc/unbind
  $ restool dprc disconnect dprc.2 --endpoint=dpni.3
  $ restool dpdmux create --default-if=1 --num-ifs=2 --method DPDMUX_METHOD_CUSTOM --manip=DPDMUX_MANIP_NONE --option=DPDMUX_OPT_CLS_MASK_SUPPORT,DPDMUX_OPT_AUTO_MAX_FRAME_LEN --container=dprc.1
  $ restool dprc connect dprc.1 --endpoint1=dpdmux.0.0 --endpoint2=dpmac.5
  $ restool dprc connect dprc.1 --endpoint1=dpdmux.0.1 --endpoint2=dpni.2
  $ restool dprc connect dprc.1 --endpoint1=dpdmux.0.2 --endpoint2=dpni.3
  $ restool dprc assign dprc.1 --object=dpdmux.0 --child=dprc.2 --plugged=1
  $ echo dprc.2 > /sys/bus/fsl-mc/drivers/vfio-fsl-mc/bind
  $ ifconfig eth0 1.1.1.2
  $ ifconfig eth0 promisc

  2. Run l3fwd application providing 'traffic-split-config' option for configuring DPDMUC rules:

  $ ./dpdk-l3fwd -l 1,2 -n 1 -- -p 0x1 --config="(0,0,1),(0,1,2)" -P --traffic-split-config="(3,2152,2)"

  Here "--traffic-split-config - (type,val,mux_conn_id)" is defined by
       'type' - 1: ETHTYPE,
		2: IP_PROTO,
		3: UDP_DST_PORT 	having value as 'val' based on which DPDMUX can
					split the traffic to mux_conn_id
		4: IP_FRAG_UDP_AND_GTP
		5: IP_FRAG_PROTO
		6: IP_FRAG_UDP_AND_GTP_AND_ESP

  Note: e.g. w.r.t. mux_conn_id:
	dpdmux.0.2 (for dpni.3) = > mux_conn_id should be '2' means => Send the specified filtered traffic to dpdk
	dpdmux.0.1 (for dpni.1) = > mux_conn_id should be '1' means => Send the specified filtered traffic to linux

  IPsec Gateway 'dpdk-ipsec-secgw' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  For IPsec application, two DUTs need to be configured as endpoint 0 (ep0) and endpoint 1 (ep1). Assuming that endpoint
  have 4 ports each:
   - Connect Port 1 and Port 1 of the ep0 and ep1 to each other (back-to-back).
   - Connect Port 0 and Port 0 of the ep0 and ep1 to packet generator (for example, Spirent).

  The sample configurations for both ep0 and ep1 are available at "nxp/ipsec".
  Custom port mappings, SA/SP and the routes can be configured in the corresponding
  configuration file named as ep0.cfg and ep1.cfg for respective endpoint.

  Endpoint0 Command:
  -----------------
  * Example command for 4 cores and 4 ports:
  dpdk-ipsec-secgw -l 0-3 -n 1 -- -p 0xf -P -u 0xa --config="(0,0,0),(1,0,1),(2,0,2),(3,0,3)" -f
  ep0_64X64_proto.cfg

  Endpoint1 Command:
  -----------------
  * Example command for 4 cores and 4 ports:
  dpdk-ipsec-secgw -l 0-3 -n 1 -- -p 0xf -P -u 0xa --config="(0,0,0),(1,0,1),(2,0,2),(3,0,3)" -f
  ep1_64X64_proto.cfg

  IPsec Gateway 'dpdk-ipsec-secgw' Application using eventdev
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Execute following commands to run 'dpdk-ipsec-secgw' application with eventdev

  Note:
    1. For DPAA1 platforms

	-> disable push mode queue, using 'export DPAA_PUSH_QUEUES_NUMBER=0'

	-> virtual device should be "event_dpaa1" i.e. --vdev="event_dpaa1"

    2. For DPAA2 platforms

	-> virtual device should be "event_dpaa2" i.e. --vdev="event_dpaa2"

  -- With parallel queue configuration

  $ ./dpdk-ipsec-secgw -l 2,3 --vdev="event_dpaa1" --vdev="event_dpaa1" -- -p 0x30 -P -u 0x20 -f ./ep0_64X64_proto.cfg --transfer-mode=event --event-schedule-type=parallel

  -- With atomic queue configuration

  $ ./dpdk-ipsec-secgw -l 2,3 --vdev="event_dpaa1" --vdev="event_dpaa1" -- -p 0x30 -P -u 0x20 -f ./ep0_64X64_proto.cfg --transfer-mode=event --event-schedule-type=atomic

  -- With ordered queue configuration

  $ ./dpdk-ipsec-secgw -l 2,3 --vdev="event_dpaa1" --vdev="event_dpaa1" -- -p 0x30 -P -u 0x20 -f ./ep0_64X64_proto.cfg --transfer-mode=event --event-schedule-type=ordered

  Note:
      * Above commands are for the 1st endpoint, to run on 2nd endpoint just change the configuration file "ep0_64X64_proto.cfg" to "ep1_64X64_proto.cfg"
      * There is no queue configuration for event mode. Only single QP is supported which will be shared by all the cores.

  Layer-2 Forwarding 'dpdk-l2fwd-event' Application
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Execute following commands to run 'dpdk-l2fwd-event' application with eventdev

  Note:
    1. For DPAA1 platforms

	-> disable push mode queue, using 'export DPAA_PUSH_QUEUES_NUMBER=0'

	-> virtual device should be "event_dpaa1" i.e. --vdev="event_dpaa1"

    2. For DPAA2 platforms

	-> virtual device should be "event_dpaa2" i.e. --vdev="event_dpaa2"

    3. Only single instance of virtual device(vdev) is allowed.

  -- With parallel queue configuration

  For 1 core, 1 Port, 1 queue per port=>

  $ dpdk-l2fwd-event -l 1,4 -n 1 --vdev=event_dpaa2 -- -p 0x1 -T 0 --mode=eventdev -q 1 --eventq-sched=parallel

  -- With atomic queue configuration

  For 1 core, 1 Port, 1 queue per port=>

  $ dpdk-l2fwd-event -l 1,4 -n 1 --vdev=event_dpaa2 -- -p 0x1 -T 0 --mode=eventdev -q 1 --eventq-sched=atomic

  -- With ordered queue configuration

  For 1 core, 1 Port, 1 queue per port=>

  $ dpdk-l2fwd-event -l 1,4 -n 1 --vdev=event_dpaa2 -- -p 0x1 -T 0 --mode=eventdev -q 1 --eventq-sched=ordered

  Layer-3 Forwarding 'dpdk-l3fwd' Application using eventdev
  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

  Execute following commands to run 'l3fwd' application with eventdev

  Note:
    1. For DPAA1 platforms

	-> disable push mode queue, using 'export DPAA_PUSH_QUEUES_NUMBER=0'

	-> virtual device should be "event_dpaa1" i.e. --vdev="event_dpaa1"

    2. For DPAA2 platforms

	-> virtual device should be "event_dpaa2" i.e. --vdev="event_dpaa2"

    3. Only single instance of virtual device(vdev) is allowed.

  -- With parallel queue configuration

  For 1 core, 1 Port, 1 queue per port=>

  $ dpdk-l3fwd -l 1 -n 1 --vdev="event_dpaa2" -- -p 0x1 --mode=eventdev --eventq-sched=parallel --event-eth-rxqs=1 -P

  -- With atomic queue configuration

  For 1 core, 1 Port, 1 queue per port=>

  $ dpdk-l3fwd -l 1 -n 1 --vdev="event_dpaa2" -- -p 0x1 --mode=eventdev --eventq-sched=atomic --event-eth-rxqs=1 -P

  -- With ordered queue configuration

  For 1 core, 1 Port, 1 queue per port=>

  $ dpdk-l3fwd -l 1 -n 1 --vdev="event_dpaa2" -- -p 0x1 --mode=eventdev --eventq-sched=ordered --event-eth-rxqs=1 -P

================================================================================
Executing dpdk-pdump/dpdk-procinfo on DPAA2
===========================================

Both, 'dpdk-pdump' and 'dpdk-procinfo' are examples of secondary applications
which run along a primary DPDK process. Being different processes, rather than
thread, they work over the DPDK EAL's Multiprocess infrastructure to communicate
with the primary process.

`dpdk-pdump` for capturing packets
----------------------------------

DPDK Supports executing secondary processes/applications which can query
the primary application (the first one) for information. For example, the
sample application 'dpdk-pdump' allows capturing of packets arriving on
other interfaces/devices available in DPDK.

The libpcap-based PMD has an external dependency on the libpcap development files
which must be installed on the board, if its not already installed.

	$ wget https://www.tcpdump.org/release/libpcap-1.10.1.tar.gz
	$ tar -xzvf libpcap-1.10.1.tar.gz
	$ cd libpcap-1.10.1
	$ ./configure --host=aarch64-linux-gnu --with-pcap=linux
	$ make install

To check if the library is installed:
	$ ldconfig -p | grep libpcap

Compiling 'dpdk-pdump':

Steps to cross compile libpcap:
------------------------------------------------
	$ git clone https://github.com/the-tcpdump-group/libpcap.git
	$ cd libpcap
	$ git checkout libpcap-1.10.1 -b libpcap-1.10.1
	$ ./configure --host=aarch64-linux-gnu --with-pcap=linux --prefix=<Path to install pcap directory>
	$ make install

Steps to cross compile dpdk-pdump:
------------------------------------------------
	Meson depends on pkgconfig to find the dependencies. The package pkg-config-aarch64-linux-gnu
	is required for aarch64. To install it in Ubuntu:
	$ sudo apt install pkg-config-aarch64-linux-gnu

	$ export PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR=$PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR:<Path to installed pcap directory>/lib
	$ export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=$PKG_CONFIG_PATH:<Path to installed pcap directory>/lib/pkgconfig
	$ pkg-config --modversion libpcap	#Verify pcap libraries can be used using pkgconfig.
	$ meson arm64-build --cross-file config/arm/arm64_dpaa_linux_gnu_gcc
	NOTE: For i.MX-95 & i.MX-93 [A55 platforms]:
		meson arm64-build --cross-file config/arm/arm64_imx_linux_gnu_gcc
	$ ninja -C arm64-build


	NOTE: "--reconfigure" in meson may not work after updating
	      PKG_CONFIG_PATH and PKG_CONFIG_LIBDIR. Better you delete the meson
	      build folder and recreate it to use the updated paths.

'dpdk-pdump'` would be available in the arm64-build/app folder along with 'testpmd'
and other inbuilt DPDK apps.

Executing 'dpdk-pdump':
    Pre-condition: disable ASLR:

    $ echo 0 > /proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space

    Note: As of now only testpmd application supports dpdk-pdump;

    Step 1: Execute testpmd using routine commands

    $ dpdk-testpmd -l 0-7 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x3 --nb-ports=2

    Step 2: Get testpmd ready for IO by selecting the mode

    $ testpmd> set fwd io
    $ testpmd> start

    Step 3: Execute dpdk-pdump on a different terminal:

    $ dpdk-pdump -n 1 -- --pdump "port=0,queue=*,rx-dev=./rx.pcap"

    In the above command, "port=0, ..." parameters convey `dpdk-pdump` app
    that capture should be done on port 0 (for example, dpni.1) and packets
    being received on all queues "queue=*". Further, all the captured packets
    can be dumped to a PCAP file using "rx-dev=<path to pcap file>.

    In the above example, only Rx'd packets are being written to PCAP. For
    Tx'd packets, use something similar to "port=0,queue=*,tx-dev=./..." where
    the output PCAP file is different from Rx'd packets. Both, Rx and Tx,
    options can be simultaneously provided.

    Step 4: Stop 'dpdk-pdump' using Ctrl+C and copy the pcap file for reading
    through external application like Wireshark

Executing `dpdk-procinfo`:

    'dpdk-procinfo' is an inbuilt application which allows dumping information
    like memory and statistics of a running (primary) DPDK application. It is
    available in the app/ folder just like testpmd and dpdk-pdump.

    Note: This can work with any other DPDK application (sample application
    like l3fwd) unlike the restriction for 'dpdk-pdump'.

    Pre-condition: disable ASLR:

    $ echo 0 > /proc/sys/kernel/randomize_va_space

    Step 1: Execute any DPDK application using routine commands. For example,
    testpmd

    $ testpmd -l 0-7 -n 1 -- -i --portmask=0x3 --nb-ports=2

    Step 2: Execute 'dpdk-procinfo' on a separate terminal:

    $ dpdk-procinfo -- -m

    Above command would dump to screen the memory layout of the primary DPDK
    application. There are other switches also available like '-s' which can
    dump the statistics.

    Note: dpdk-procinfo will work without providing blocklist only if there is
    single primary process running (no other secondary). If you are using multi
    process application, you will need to provide blacklist to dpdk-procinfo.

===============================================================================

Building Pktgen Application
======================================

Pktgen is a packet generator powered by DPDK. It requires DPDK environment for
compilation and DPDK compliant infrastructure for execution. DPAA and DPAA2 DPDK
PMD (Poll Mode Drivers) can be used by Pktgen for building a packet generator
using the DPAA infrastructure.

Steps to compile pktgen on host:
----------------------------------

These steps assumes that compiled DPDK binaries are available in DPDK build
directory. Refer the section "`dpdk-pdump` for capturing packets"
for compilation steps of DPDK on host.

	$ export RTE_SDK=<path to compiled DPDK source code containing build folder>
	$ export RTE_TARGET=arm64-build
	$ export PKG_CONFIG_PATH=<path to meson-private in arm64-build folder>
	$ git clone -b  pktgen-26.02.4 https://github.com/pktgen/pktgen-dpdk.git 
	$ cd pktgen-dpdk/
	$ meson build -Dc_args="-DRTE_FORCE_INTRINSICS"
	$ ninja -C build install

Pktgen app location: pktgen-dpdk/build/app/pktgen
Pktgen Pktgen.lua location: pktgen-dpdk/Pktgen.lua

Pktgen Tag being used: pktgen-26.02.4

